the wandering  souls  of unhappy women unrighteous men priests  who have broken their vow of chastity  or even those souls who escaped  purgatory  in fact the ethereal light gleans its  most enduring name  willow the wisp or will with a wisp from  the saxon  word while for fraud trick or deceit and  the swedish word wisp  meaning a small lit bundle of tinder  variations include will with the wisp  willy wisp and will owe the wikes  essentially the name hints that the  light comes from some trickster spirit  holding a flame aloft  further north in norwegian folklore the  will of the wisp is called  haberdee's lantern hobbenese lantern or  even  hob and his lantern hob may come from  the norwegian  hopa meaning mare or the old norse word  hapa  which translates to leap or hop which  might not be as obvious a name as a  deceitful bundle of tinder  until you know that the will of the wisp  movement often resembles the cantering  motion of a horse yet another moniker  for the floating lights  jackie lantern or jack-o-lantern yes  that's right the carved pumpkins that  adorn many a stoop or doorstep on  halloween  actually borrow their name from this  mysterious light  jack-o'-lantern is of english language  origin and simply means  jack of the lantern so when did we start  hearing about these creatures  well the first recorded sighting of will  of the wisp is described in a 1340 text  pen by welsh poet david apgulem he wrote  that in every hollow live a hundred  rye-mouthed wisps  he names the phenomenon canulkorf welsh  for corpse candle  thereby introducing what will become  another common name for the supernatural  blue flames  and associating the lights with death  and burial sites  the first appearance of the lights in  the english language occurs in 1563  under the name ignis fatuous here they  are described as foolish fire that  hurteth not  in another variation of the will of the  wisp myth the ghost lights are  attributed to a mischievous sprite  called a puck  